On Jul 9, 2011, at 3:51 PM, Lenth, Russell V wrote:

> Simon,
> 
> Thanks --- now my stuff works! On your last comment -- suppose I started R
> using JGR to begin with; then I'm OK, right?
> 

Yes. In that case .jengine() will simply return the engine created by JGR on startup and .jinit() will be a no-op since rJava is already loaded and initialized. In fact direct REngine.getLastEngine() will succeed since JGR created that one as well ;) - so to save yourself the hassle, you can try that one first if you want ...

Cheers,
S


